A||||6||87FieroGT||Still having Code 42 error.
Z||000000||87FieroGT||05-22-99||03:08 AM||||Here is a more precise description of what goes on. I have replaced all the ignition components (distributor, wires, plugs, etc.). I have also replaced the ECM ($150 down the drain). When I first start out on a trip of about 30 miles and the car has not yet warmed up it runs beautifully through the speed range. At about 10 miles into the trip I notice that the tach needle starts to jump a little and then the check engine light comes on. If I go over 3000 RPM the engine totally cuts out. Also in this mode the idle is a little eradic. The needle will move from 1500 to 1000 and back and forth. Coul it have anything to do with the electronics of the fuel delivery? Or should I break down and take it somewhere so a scanner can diagnose the problem? I really want to get rid of this problem forever.
Z||000001||theogre||05-22-99||05:30 PM||||I forgot.... did you take out the tach filter and run it?<p>If you did. try another coil. cutout at higher RPM is a typical symptom of the coil not charging. You've already replaced most of what ever else there is connected to it by the sound of things. You can find a used one on almost any GM 4cyl product from that time period.<p>Did you carfully check all the engine wiring? Including all the ground lugs? (there's more than one wire grounding things to the block.)
Z||000002||FieroMike||05-23-99||01:54 PM||||87FieroGT,<br>It could possibly be the fuel pump.  My dads GrandAm did the same thing and he finally replace the fuel pump and filter and that fixed the problem.  The pump may work for a while and then as the car runs longer the motor starts to fail. Just an idea.
Z||000003||87GT||05-23-99||09:25 PM||||I just got my Fiero and the only thing I noticed that is a bit unusual, is that it does act hesitent to get going in the morning. The tach jumps around a some.  Once I get the car going down the road, lets say 5 miles, it doesnt do it anymore.  It acts as if nothing ever happened.  Im going to have it checked out though. 
Z||000004||87FieroGT||05-27-99||06:33 PM||||I got it working!!!!!  After going into the engine compartment and unplugging the connections to the ignition coil and then plugging it back in, and also just moving around some of the wiring harness the problem stopped. The car still doesn't idle perfect sometimes, but the problem went away hopefully for good.
Z||000005||RFMike||05-28-99||06:31 PM||||Not likely.  Intermitent electrical problems always seem to come back sooner or later.  Reminds me of the 1973 Norton I had.  The electronics were made by Lucas Corporation.  This company was nicknamed "Lucas, Prince of Darkness", because about the time you thought you had all of the shorts solved, you'd be 100 miles from home on a moonless night, and the headlight would go out.  The next afternoon, after you got it home, it would be perfect.  Hope this isn't the case for you, but wiggling wires doesn't sound like a permanent fix.  Sorry for raining on your parade.<br>Mike
Z||000006||theogre||05-28-99||09:06 PM||||if that's what fixed it........ take all those terminals apart again, and carelfully inspect the wire. make sure there are not any breaks right next to the terminal inside the insulation. If the wire is good clean the connections, and smear them with a bit of silicon dielectric grease before reassembling them.<p>If all they are is dirty, this will fix them peranantly.
